# Copy to deploy/.env and fill the master key in. Compose reads this file
# automatically when you run `docker compose` from this directory, so the same
# values are used on every boot — which matters more than it looks: see below.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# deploy/.env is gitignored. Keep it; it is not reconstructible.


# There is no login secret here. People sign in with an account (docs/AUTH.md);
# the YAAF_ADMIN_TOKEN this file used to carry was retired and nothing reads it.
#
# The first admin's email, for the deploy and update scripts to make that
# account the admin on a box that has none yet. Optional — they also take
# --admin-email, and ask when run interactively.
YAAF_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# The key that envelope-encrypts every stored secret (API keys, MCP URLs,
# connector credentials). This one is NOT free-form: it must be a Fernet key,
# 32 url-safe base64 bytes *with* padding. Either of these produces one:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#   python3 -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
#
# What does NOT work is `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)`, the obvious Python idiom
# — it strips the base64 padding, so it looks right and then fails at the
# first secret you store.
#
# Two ways to lose every secret you have stored:
#   - change this value  — the old ciphertext no longer decrypts
#   - leave it unset     — compose substitutes an empty value and the server
#                          refuses to read or write secrets at all
# There is no recovery path for either. Back this up somewhere you trust.
YAAF_MASTER_KEY=

# The address people type to reach the dashboard, scheme included:
#
#   YAAF_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://yaaf.example.com/
#
# Use the https:// address your proxy serves, not the container's own port.
# Two things read it. Session cookies are marked Secure only when it starts
# with https. And yaaf's MCP/OAuth discovery documents advertise it as the
# issuer and endpoints, so a connector such as claude.ai cannot register when
# it is unset (they then name http://api:8080, which resolves only inside
# compose) or names a host it cannot reach. Changing it later changes the MCP
# audience: connected OAuth sources have to be connected again. Settings are
# read at container start, so `docker compose up -d` after editing.
# Leave it empty for a box only you reach on its own port.
YAAF_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=


# ── chairman notifications (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The email you put on a project is the chairman's identity either way. Fill
# these in and it also becomes an inbox: approvals waiting on you, assists,
# budget pauses and run deaths arrive there instead of only in the logs.
#
# The transport is plain SMTP with STARTTLS, so use the submission port (587),
# not implicit-TLS 465 — 465 expects the handshake before the greeting and
# will hang until the 15s timeout. Leave USERNAME empty for a relay on your own
# network that does not authenticate; set it and PASSWORD for a real provider.
#
# Gmail wants an App Password, not your account password:
#   YAAF_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
#   YAAF_SMTP_USERNAME=you@gmail.com
#   YAAF_SMTP_PASSWORD=<16-char app password>
#   YAAF_SMTP_FROM=you@gmail.com
#
# FROM defaults to USERNAME, then to yaaf@<host>. Most providers reject a From
# they do not own, so set it to an address the account is allowed to send as.
# YAAF_SMTP_HOST=
# YAAF_SMTP_PORT=587
# YAAF_SMTP_USERNAME=
# YAAF_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# YAAF_SMTP_FROM=

# Anything that takes a JSON POST with a "text" field — a Slack incoming
# webhook is the usual one. Independent of SMTP; set either, both or neither.
# YAAF_CHAIRMAN_WEBHOOK_URL=


# ── a second stack on the same box (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Leave all four unset for a normal single-stack box: the defaults below are
# exactly what shipped before they existed, so nothing moves.
#
# Set them to stand a second stack beside a live one — a git worktree testing a
# branch, or a staging deploy. Each is a real collision, and three of the four
# fail silently rather than loudly:
#
#   YAAF_STACK=modes
#     Names the compose project, so containers and the Postgres volume are the
#     twin's own. Without it both stacks are called "deploy" (the directory
#     name, identical in every worktree) and the second one adopts the first's
#     containers and database. Also labels every sandbox and chat container, so
#     teardown in one stack cannot reap runs in the other.
#
#   YAAF_STATE_ROOT=/var/lib/yaaf-modes
#     Workspaces, artifacts and generated squid config. These are bind mounts
#     from fixed host paths that two runners would otherwise both write.
#
#   YAAF_SANDBOX_NETWORK=yaaf_sandbox_modes
#     The sandbox network is declared with an explicit name, so compose does
#     NOT prefix it per project. Shared, `compose down` in one stack removes it
#     under the other — and sandboxes resolve the server by the hostname `api`,
#     so a run in one stack can reach the other stack's API.
#
#   YAAF_SANDBOX_IMAGE=yaaf-sandbox:modes
#     Build the sandbox image under the shared tag and the LIVE stack's next
#     run uses your branch's code. No restart, no signal — the tag is read per
#     run. Build the twin's with:
#       docker build -f deploy/sandbox/Dockerfile -t yaaf-sandbox:modes .
#
# Pick the dashboard port with `deploy/deploy_single_server.sh --port 8081`; that one
# already fails loudly, so it needs no variable here.
#
# Keep YAAF_MASTER_KEY IDENTICAL to the stack you are twinning if you plan to
# restore a dump of its database — the stored secrets are encrypted with it and
# will not decrypt under a different one.
# YAAF_STACK=
# YAAF_STATE_ROOT=
# YAAF_SANDBOX_NETWORK=
# YAAF_SANDBOX_IMAGE=
